{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"- Unblock Context7 rate limits first, then validate your latency policy against current OpenAI docs before rollout.  \n- Stream responses (`stream: true`) and render tokens immediately to cut perceived turn latency.  \n- Reuse persistent HTTP connections (keep-alive) and avoid per-turn client reinitialization/TLS handshakes.  \n- Cap generation length (`max_output_tokens`) and tighten instructions to reduce unnecessary output tokens.  \n- Keep prompts short: move stable instructions to a cached/static prefix and trim per-turn conversation context aggressively.  \n- Maintain rolling conversation summaries so each turn sends only the minimal recent window plus summary.  \n- Run retrieval/tool calls in parallel and send one final model call, instead of chaining multiple sequential model calls.  \n- Collapse “classify + answer” into a single structured-output call when possible to avoid extra round trips.  \n- Implement fast-path routing: send low-risk/simple turns to a small, fast model and escalate to a stronger model only on low confidence, safety trigger, or explicit complexity signals.  \n- Define hard router thresholds (confidence, intent class, required tool use, safety flags) and log route decisions for tuning.  \n- Enforce a per-turn latency budget with staged timeouts (retrieve, tool, model) and fail early when a stage overruns.  \n- Use safe fallback policy: one bounded retry with jitter, then fallback model; keep idempotency keys to prevent duplicate side effects.  \n- Restrict fallback to model substitutions only; never auto-replay non-idempotent external tool actions.  \n- Add circuit breakers (temporary downgrade to fast model) when primary model latency/error rate crosses thresholds.  \n- Measure p50/p95/p99 separately for router, retrieval, tool, and model stages; optimize the slowest stage first."}}
